WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Fertilisation is the fusion of haploid gametes to form a diploid zygote — the start of a new organism.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:28.000
In humans, millions of sperm travel toward a single oocyte still wrapped in protective layers after ovulation.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Sperm must reach the cumulus cells and the zona pellucida, a glycoprotein coat around the egg.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:55.000
Capacitation in the female tract prepares sperm membranes so they can undergo the acrosome reaction.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:50.000
The acrosome releases enzymes that digest a path through the zona pellucida so the sperm can reach the egg membrane.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Membrane fusion lets the sperm nucleus enter. The cortical reaction hardens the zona and blocks other sperm.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Male and female pronuclei — each haploid — come together. Their chromosomes align for the first mitosis.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:06:00.000
The zygote is diploid and totipotent. Cleavage divisions follow without much growth between mitoses.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Remember: approach, acrosome reaction, fusion, block to polyspermy, then a diploid zygote ready for development.
